The '''Abyala Charter''' (1537), formally the '''Charter of Division of the Lands of Abyala''', was a bilateral treaty between the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] and the '''[[United Kingdoms of Volisania]]''' that divided the continent of [[Abyala]] into two spheres of colonial jurisdiction. The charter designated the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]], the mountain chain running along the eastern seaboard of Abyala, as the principal boundary: territories east of the mountains were assigned to Burawa, and territories west to the United Kingdoms. Where the range ended, the boundary was to follow the same longitudinal line southward to the coast.

The treaty was brokered jointly by the [[Pope]] and the [[Cardinal of Burawa]], signed at the [[Papal States]] on the western coast of the [[Terdolian Peninsula]], and witnessed by representatives of both Churches. The charter established the framework for colonial governance in Abyala for over a century. Its principal weakness — the ambiguity of the boundary south of the mountains, where the line's precise course was poorly communicated between the colonial territories and the metropolitan courts — became a source of sustained dispute and a contributing cause of the [[War of the Mesogean]] (c. 1590s–1610s).

== Background ==

By the 1530s, both Burawa and Volisania had established colonial presences on [[Abyala]]. The [[Kingdom of Burawa]] held the colony of [[Lacashe]], founded in 1516 on the southeastern coast, which had expanded northward into the territory collectively known as [[Burawi Cusub]]. The [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]], formed in 1412 through the union of the [[Kingdom of Volisania]] and the [[Kingdom of Berona]], controlled the colony of [[Balisca]] in the north and west of the continent. Overlapping claims and commercial disputes along the coast and in the interior threatened to produce conflict between the two powers.

The initiative for the treaty came jointly from the [[Pope]], the head of the Church in [[Illypnia]], and the '''[[Cardinal of Burawa]]''', the head of the [[Burawi Catholic Church]]. The two Churches, which were [[Christianity in Adonia|in communion]] with one another, sought to prevent a colonial war between two Christian kingdoms and to establish a framework within which the conversion of Abyala's indigenous peoples could proceed without inter-colonial conflict. The negotiations were convened at the [[Papal States]], the cluster of island city-states on the western coast of northern Volisania that served as the seat of the papacy under Volisanian protection.

The geographic knowledge available to the negotiators was derived from reports submitted by explorers and colonial officials in the field. Both Burawi and Volisanian expeditions had confirmed the existence of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] as a continuous mountain barrier running roughly parallel to the eastern coast, and the range served as the obvious basis for a territorial division. The southern portion of the continent — where both powers had first made landfall and which was therefore better known — was discussed, but the precise details of the terrain south of the mountains' terminus were poorly communicated to the metropolitan diplomats who drafted the treaty's terms.

== Terms ==

=== Territorial division ===

The charter established the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] as the boundary between the two colonial spheres. All territories east of the mountain ridge were assigned to the [[Kingdom of Burawa]]; all territories west were assigned to the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|United Kingdoms]]. South of the point where the range subsided, the boundary was to continue along the same longitudinal line to the coast. Existing settlements — [[Lacashe]] and [[Burawi Cusub]] on the eastern coast, [[Balisca]] on the western — were confirmed under the jurisdiction of their respective crowns. Neither power was permitted to establish new settlements or conduct trade within the other's designated sphere without licence.

=== Trade zones ===

The charter established exclusive trade zones corresponding to the territorial division. Burawi merchants were granted sole commercial access to the eastern coast, the foothills of the mountain range, and the interior lowlands east of the ridge. Volisanian merchants held corresponding rights in the west. The importation and exportation of goods between the two zones was subject to tariff agreements to be negotiated separately between the two crowns.

=== Indigenous peoples ===

The charter made no provision for the sovereignty, rights, or consent of the indigenous peoples of [[Abyala]]. The [[Tapuya Confederacy]], the [[Aruanãyára Empire]], and the numerous smaller polities of the interior were treated as subjects of whichever colonial power's territorial sphere they inhabited. The charter authorised both crowns to govern, tax, and convert the indigenous populations within their respective zones. The ecclesiastical witnesses — the [[Pope]] and the [[Cardinal of Burawa]] — endorsed this provision, affirming the duty of both kingdoms to bring the indigenous peoples of Abyala into the Christian faith.

=== Enforcement ===

The charter contained no mechanism for joint enforcement. Compliance was left to the good faith of both parties, with disputes to be referred to the [[Pope]] for arbitration. In practice, the distance between the metropolitan courts and the colonial territories, the incomplete communication between colonial officials and the diplomats who had drafted the treaty, and the absence of any joint colonial administration rendered enforcement largely nominal. Colonial governors on both sides exercised broad discretionary authority, and the boundary — clear in the mountainous interior — was treated with considerable flexibility in the lowlands and along the coast.

== The southern boundary dispute ==

The charter's principal deficiency concerned the territory south of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]]'s southern terminus. In the interior, where the mountains formed a continuous barrier, the boundary functioned without difficulty. South of the point where the range subsided into lowlands — approximately 100 kilometres from the coast — the charter stipulated that the boundary should continue along the same line to the sea.

The difficulty was one of communication rather than omission. The southern coast was the first region of Abyala known to both colonial powers, and its geography was not in dispute. However, the practical application of the line south of the mountains depended on precise cartographic agreement about where the range ended and where the projected line fell — details that the colonial officials on the ground had not communicated with sufficient precision and that the metropolitan negotiators had not pressed. In the absence of agreed markers, both Burawi and Volisanian colonists treated the lowland zone south of the mountains as a shared or contested space. Settlements, trading posts, and resource extraction operations from both sides overlapped in the region.

The ambiguity was manageable while relations between the two powers remained cooperative. The joint conquest of the [[Tapuya Confederacy]] in the 1560s, however, brought Burawi and Volisanian forces into direct contact in the lowland interior, and the division of former Tapuya territory along the mountain line exposed the unresolved question of the southern boundary. The dispute, compounded by the separate question of [[Kingdom of Reige|Reige]]'s political allegiance, became one of the principal causes of the [[War of the Mesogean]] (c. 1590s–1610s).

== The Lochcarrons and the charter's erosion ==

The Abyala Charter was signed under the early [[House of Lochcarron]], which had taken the Volisanian throne only five years earlier in 1532. As the Lochcarron dynasty consolidated its authority through the mid- and late 16th century, its colonial policy grew increasingly assertive. The charter's terms — negotiated in haste at the [[Papal States]] and drafted on the basis of incomplete geographic knowledge — were regarded by the Lochcarron court as a constraint inherited from the preceding [[House of Ampuria]] rather than as a binding framework for future expansion.

By the 1580s, the Lochcarrons had modernised the Volisanian navy, centralised the colonial administration, and adopted a broader interpretation of Volisanian rights in [[Abyala]]. The charter's ambiguous southern boundary was exploited, and the Lochcarron court cultivated the lords of [[Kingdom of Reige|Reige]], offering the ethnically [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] island an alternative to its personal union with [[Kingdom of Burawa|Burawa]].

The [[Kingdom of Burawa]] maintained that the charter remained in force as written. The widening gap between Burawi and Volisanian interpretations of the treaty was a principal cause of the [[War of the Mesogean]] (c. 1590s–1610s).

== Legacy ==

The Abyala Charter governed the colonial partition of Abyala for over a century and established the broad territorial framework from which the modern states of eastern and western Abyala subsequently emerged. The mountain boundary proved durable in the interior, and the colonial borders it established were largely maintained even after the War of the Mesogean confirmed the loss of [[Kingdom of Reige|Reige]].

The charter is regarded by modern historians as characteristic of the limitations of metropolitan treaty-making in the colonial era: an agreement negotiated at the [[Papal States]] by diplomats and churchmen who had never visited [[Abyala]], on the basis of geographic reports that were imprecise at critical points, with no consultation of the indigenous peoples whose lands were being divided.

